{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age following his father’s assassination in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was a significant figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nRaymond III was born in 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. His father was assassinated in 1152 by the Hashashin leaving Raymond III as the heir to the County of Tripoli. Since he was only twelve years old at the time his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he reached adulthood. Raymond III took full control of Tripoli in 1155. His early years were marked by efforts to stabilize the county and defend it against external threats including Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler who sought to expand his influence in the region.\n\n**Rule and Alliances**\n\nRaymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Crusader states. He allied with other Crusader leaders such as Bohemond III of Antioch and Amalric I of Jerusalem. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din after the Battle of Harim and spent nearly ten years in captivity. During his imprisonment his mother Hodierna and later his cousin Bohemond III managed the affairs of Tripoli. After his release in 1174 Raymond III resumed his political activities and became a key player in the internal conflicts of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Conflict with Saladin and Later Years**\n\nIn the 1180s Raymond III faced the rising power of Saladin who sought to unify Muslim forces against the Crusaders. Raymond III initially opposed Guy of Lusignan’s ascension to the throne of Jerusalem and refused to participate in Guy’s campaign against Saladin in 1187. Raymond III’s cautious approach was rooted in his desire to preserve the Crusader states rather than engage in risky military ventures. His refusal to join the campaign at the Horns of Hattin contributed to the disastrous Christian defeat by Saladin in July 1187. After the loss of Jerusalem Raymond III died in Tripoli in October 1187. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which would soon face further challenges under Muslim expansion.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Legacy**\n\nRaymond III is remembered as a pragmatic ruler who prioritized the survival of the Crusader states over aggressive military campaigns. His cautious diplomacy and alliances helped maintain stability in Tripoli during a turbulent period. Despite his captivity and political setbacks Raymond III’s leadership left a lasting impact on the region’s history. His death coincided with the decline of Crusader power in the Levant though his strategies influenced later efforts to defend the remaining Christian territories.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.3243790864944458}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.6239151358604431}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -7.950943836476654e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.010276618413627148}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.723177648382261e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.768360213347478e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.1965414136648178}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.29464033246040344}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920858014491387e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00016020445036701858}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.277263879776001}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24696463346481323}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.9788545614574105e-05}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0006743779522366822}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.000651147507596761}, {"token": "1"